Reference for the `sbc_germany` benchmark — Synthetic Business Cycle (SBC),
Shi, Xi & Xie (2025), "A Synthetic Business Cycle Approach to Counterfactual
Analysis with Nonstationary Macroeconomic Data," arXiv:2505.22388.
Authors' code: github.com/jinxi-atlas/Synthetic-business-cycle-code (no licence).

Files
- reference.R       mlsynth's runner: the authors' SBC method (lsq Hamilton
                    detrending + trend_predict forecast + Synth::synth on the
                    cyclical / level components), reproduced from Germany.R with
                    the plotting removed, run on the authoritative
                    basedata/german_reunification.csv.
- golden_steps.R    Emits per-step golden values (treated/donor cycles, trend
                    coefficients, trend forecast, and the authors' Synth weights
                    + cyclical SSE) on basedata/repgermany.dta, for the unit
                    tests in mlsynth/tests/test_sbc_reference.py.
- golden_steps.txt  Captured output of golden_steps.R (committed so the tests
                    need no R toolchain). Each compared quantity appears twice:
                    the original %.8f line, and a "hi:" line at %.17g. The
                    tests read the "hi:" line and compare at one unit in its
                    last recorded place, so the capture -- not a hand-set
                    constant -- decides how tight the comparison is. The %.8f
                    form resolves 5e-9, three orders coarser than the real
                    difference between the implementations, so on its own it
                    could not measure their agreement. The "hi:" lines were
                    added on 2026-08-23 with R 4.3.3 from
                    basedata/german_reunification.csv, after checking that the
                    same run reproduces the committed %.8f lines byte for byte.
- reference.json / reference.out / provenance.json
                    Captured run of reference.R (the authors' Synth::synth
                    output, version-pinned).

What the cross-validation found
- mlsynth's Hamilton detrending and trend forecast reproduce the authors' lsq /
  trend_predict to 1.7e-14 of each series' scale (~1e-11 absolute), the distance
  between R's lm QR (dqrls) and numpy's lstsq (gelsd) on the same design. Two
  least-squares kernels do not agree bit for bit, so this is as close as the two
  can come.
- The synthetic-control step is the one divergence, and mlsynth is the more
  accurate implementation. The cyclical simplex least-squares is strictly convex
  and well-conditioned (donor matrix full rank, Gram condition number ~3.8e3),
  so its optimum is unique. mlsynth's FISTA and cvxpy's ECOS and CLARABEL agree
  on it (SSE 1266162.58, weights to 2.7e-6); OSQP agrees once its answer is
  projected back onto the simplex, and SCS at its default tolerance returns an
  infeasible point (a weight of -4.5e-3) whose objective is therefore lower than
  the optimum's, and 0.7% worse once projected. The check the tests assert is
  mlsynth's own optimality certificate (1.4e-6 relative, from the convexity of
  the objective), which needs no solver to be trusted. The authors' Synth::synth
  (kernlab ipop) sits at a cyclical SSE ~2.6% higher, and tightening ipop's
  tolerances does not close the gap. So the case pins mlsynth's verified optimum, NOT the
  authors' Synth output.
- The upstream repo's shipped wide Germany.csv permutes its donor column labels
  in fifteen of sixteen columns -- only Italy sits under its own name. Against
  the canonical repgermany.dta the mapping is: Australia<-USA, Austria<-UK,
  Belgium<-Austria, Denmark<-Belgium, France<-Denmark, Greece<-France,
  Japan<-Netherlands, Netherlands<-Norway, New Zealand<-Switzerland,
  Norway<-Japan, Portugal<-Greece, Spain<-Portugal, Switzerland<-Spain,
  UK<-Australia, US<-New Zealand (values identical to the last digit; only the
  names move). So the paper's printed cycle donors ("Italy, Japan, Portugal")
  are, correctly labelled, Italy / Netherlands / Greece -- the optimum mlsynth
  recovers -- and its trend donors ("Australia, Belgium") are the USA and
  Austria, the two donors mlsynth's level-matching weights load.

The authors' code/data are reproduced here for offline replication of this
benchmark only; all credit to the authors.
